WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.688
Saturated tanh, and gradients that vanish.

2
00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:05.656
The hidden layer is tanh of a pre-activation.

3
00:00:05.936 --> 00:00:10.736
If the pre-activations are large, most tanh outputs sit at plus or minus 1.

4
00:00:11.016 --> 00:00:15.304
The derivative of tanh is 1 minus t squared, so at t = 0.

5
00:00:15.584 --> 00:00:20.533
99 the unit passes back only about 2 percent of the gradient it receives.

6
00:00:20.813 --> 00:00:28.067
A unit that is saturated for every example in the batch passes back essentially nothing: its incoming weights stop learning.

7
00:00:28.347 --> 00:00:40.784
The lecture’s white-pixel plot (12:59) shows units against examples, white wherever the output is very close to plus or minus 1, and a column of all white is a dead neuron.

8
00:00:41.064 --> 00:00:43.112
Other nonlinearities fail differently.

9
00:00:43.392 --> 00:00:56.832
ReLU has derivative exactly 0 for negative inputs, so a ReLU unit whose pre-activation is negative for all inputs is permanently dead (a large learning rate can knock units into that state).

10
00:00:57.112 --> 00:00:59.480
Sigmoid saturates at both ends like tanh.

11
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.563
Every squashing nonlinearity has a flat region, and the question is how much of the data lands there.

12
00:01:05.843 --> 00:01:14.397
The fix is again a matter of scale: initialize the first layer so pre-activations have a standard deviation around 1, not 10.

13
00:01:14.677 --> 00:01:18.155
Which raises the question: what scale is right, in general?
